        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Masonry Defender is not one sealer. It is a line from SaverSystems, a chimney-trade supplier, that mixes breathable silane-siloxane water repellents with film-forming acrylic sealers that add sheen and darken the surface. Buying the right one matters more than the brand, so this review sorts them out using the maker's own data sheets.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="who-makes-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Who Makes Masonry Defender
          </h2>

          <p>
            Masonry Defender is made by SaverSystems of Richmond, Indiana, the company behind the ChimneySaver water repellents sold through chimney professionals. Industry directories date SaverSystems to 1985, when it started supplying chimney and masonry products to the chimney sweep trade. Masonry Defender is its homeowner line, and the brand's own site points buyers to Amazon rather than to a dealer network.
          </p>

          <p>
            That background is the best argument for the brand. A company that has spent decades making water repellents for chimney contractors knows the problems that matter on vertical masonry: freeze-thaw spalling, water driven into porous brick, and sealers that trap moisture. The chimney-focused products in the Masonry Defender line come straight from that experience.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is that the brand describes itself as a complete line made with silane/siloxane and acrylic technologies, and those are opposite approaches. A silane-siloxane repellent soaks into the masonry, leaves no film and lets water vapour out. An acrylic sealer forms a film on the surface, adds sheen and darkens the material. Both carry the same Masonry Defender label, so the product name on the jug matters more than the brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-brick-sealer-reviews/">brick sealer rankings</a> include Masonry Defender's penetrating brick sealer as a value pick. This review goes further into the line, separates the penetrating products from the film-formers, and looks at what the warranty actually promises.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Masonry Defender Lineup, Product by Product
          </h2>

          <p>
            The table covers the products whose data sheets we could read. Coverage and warranty figures are the maker's. The brand also sells gloss paver, stamped concrete, chimney crown and chimney flashing products, which are not covered here.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Chemistry</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coverage per gal</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Warranty</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">All Purpose Concrete, Brick & Stone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water-based silane-siloxane, no sheen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Exterior above-grade masonry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">80-150 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 yrs, vertical</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chimney Brick Sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water-based modified siloxane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Exterior chimneys</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">80-150 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 yrs, vertical chimney</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete Driveway Sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Silane/siloxane blend</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Driveways, patios, walks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90-150 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 yrs, horizontal</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Interior/Exterior Brick Sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water-based acrylic, slight satin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Interior or exterior vertical brick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">80-150 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not stated</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Low Gloss Patio Paver Sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water-based acrylic, slight wet look</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clay and concrete pavers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">75-200 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 yrs, horizontal</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Advanced Water Repellent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polysiloxane, zero VOC</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vertical and horizontal masonry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90-150 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 yrs, vertical</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The penetrating products are the All Purpose Concrete, Brick & Stone Sealer, the Chimney Brick Sealer, the Concrete Driveway Sealer and the Advanced Water Repellent. Their data sheets describe them as breathable and non-film-forming, and say they will not change the colour or appearance of most surfaces, with slight darkening possible on dense ones. The All Purpose sheet lists VOC content under 50 g/L and the Chimney Brick sheet 50 g/L. The Chimney Brick Sealer is described as penetrating up to 1/4 inch.
          </p>

          <p>
            The film-formers are the Interior/Exterior Brick Sealer and the Patio Paver Sealer, and both are acrylic. The brick sealer goes on milky, dries clear with a slight satin sheen, and the maker says it will darken the surface. The paver sealer dries to a low-gloss, slight wet look, also darkens the surface, and claims to bond joint sand. If you want brick or pavers to look untouched, these are not the products to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Advanced Water Repellent is the newest. Its data sheet describes a polysiloxane formula that can be applied over existing water- or solvent-based repellents, with zero VOCs and a 7-year vertical warranty, though the brand's site has listed it as coming soon. Check availability before planning around it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How the Penetrating Products Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Silanes are small molecules that soak deep into the pores of brick, concrete and stone. Siloxanes are larger and stay closer to the surface. Once inside, both react with the minerals in the masonry and leave the pore walls lined with a water-repellent layer. The pores stay open, so water vapour from inside the wall can still escape while liquid water beads on the outside. Our <a href="/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/">silane vs siloxane guide</a> explains why blends are common.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two details in the Masonry Defender data sheets are worth knowing before you test the result. The chimney and Advanced products say the surface becomes water repellent after 4 to 6 hours of exposure to UV light and atmospheric moisture, and that the repellency increases in strength for several weeks after treatment. So a splash test the same afternoon can be disappointing. Wait at least a day, and judge the full effect after a few weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Breathability is what makes this chemistry right for chimneys and exterior walls. Moisture inside a chimney, from flue gases, condensation or a leaking crown, has to escape somewhere. A film-forming sealer can trap it behind the face of the brick, where freeze-thaw cycles push the surface off. A penetrating repellent lets that moisture leave through the pores while stopping rain from soaking in.
          </p>

          <p>
            What these products will not do is just as important. The data sheets say they will not stop water coming through structural cracks, open joints or defects, and the warranty excludes pre-existing flaking, spalling and faulty construction. If mortar joints are eroded or a crown is cracked, repair those first. Sealing a wall with failed joints only waterproofs the brick faces while water still gets in through the gaps.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What the Masonry Defender Warranty Covers
          </h2>

          <p>
            The warranty is better than many in the category in one respect: it is a performance warranty rather than a defects-only one. The All Purpose sheet says SaverSystems warrants that the sealer will exhibit a water repellent effect on vertical concrete or masonry for five years from the date of application. The Chimney Brick sheet gives the same five years on vertical chimney surfaces, and the Advanced sheet gives seven.
          </p>

          <p>
            The conditions are where it narrows. You must register on masonrydefender.com with a copy of your receipt for the warranty to activate. The product must be applied according to the label and meet the minimum application requirements, which means hitting the coverage rate rather than misting the wall. If the repellent fails, the remedy is enough water repellent to correct the condition, and the warranty explicitly excludes labour. On a two-storey chimney, labour or scaffolding is most of the cost, so the practical value is limited.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Register the day you apply.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">No registration and receipt, no warranty. Photograph the receipt and the jug's batch details, register online, and note the application date somewhere you will find it in five years.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Horizontal surfaces get shorter terms. The Concrete Driveway Sealer and the Patio Paver Sealer each carry three years on horizontal surfaces, which reflects the abrasion, standing water and de-icing salt that flat surfaces face. The paver sealer's warranty also requires both steps of the maker's two-step system, meaning the Masonry Defender cleaner followed by the sealer. Using a different cleaner may give the maker grounds to refuse a claim.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Application Rules From the Data Sheets
          </h2>

          <p>
            Conditions for the penetrating products are more forgiving than for most film-formers. Surface and air temperatures should be between 40°F and 100°F for three hours after application, the surface should be clean, sound and dry to slightly damp, and no rain should be expected for six hours. Do not apply to frozen or frost-covered masonry. The acrylic paver sealer is stricter: 50°F to 90°F surface temperature, a thoroughly dry surface and no rain for eight hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Preparation follows a set order. Clean with the Masonry Defender All Purpose Masonry & Concrete Cleaner or an equivalent, rinse, and let the surface dry for at least six hours. New masonry needs to cure for 28 days, and repairs or tuckpointing need at least three days. Remove stains first, because the data sheets warn that the sealer will darken any stain left on the surface. Protect windows, plants, painted trim and asphalt from overspray, and wipe any overspray off right away with soap and water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vertical surfaces are treated from the bottom up with a low-pressure sprayer at 10 to 25 psi with a fan nozzle, or a roller with at least a 3/8-inch nap. The instruction is to flood the surface until the excess runs six to eight inches below the spray pattern, then apply a second coat wet-on-wet within three to five minutes. Horizontal surfaces get a single application, kept wet for two to three minutes, with puddles brushed out. Over-application can darken the surface, so do not keep adding product to a wall that has stopped absorbing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect the surface to be dry in two to six hours and allow 24 hours before driving on a treated driveway. The All Purpose and driveway products are for exterior, above-grade use only and not for asphalt, painted surfaces or below-grade walls. If your driveway is asphalt, you need an asphalt emulsion sealer instead, like those in our <a href="/henry-driveway-sealer-review/">Henry driveway sealer review</a>. Do not store any of the products below 32°F.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">From the Maker's Data Sheets</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">40-100°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Application window, penetrating products</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-5 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Wet-on-wet second coat on walls</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">80-150</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per gallon, typical</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">28 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cure for new masonry</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="versus" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Masonry Defender vs Foundation Armor, LastiSeal and ChimneySaver
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Vs Foundation Armor SX5000.</strong> Both are silane-siloxane penetrating repellents. The SX5000 is solvent-based, with a water-based WB version, and is sold on a high active content, as our <a href="/foundation-armor-sx5000-reviews/">SX5000 review</a> covers. Masonry Defender's penetrating products are water-based with VOC at or under 50 g/L, so they are easier to use near a house and clean up with soap and water. If you want the strongest concrete treatment and can handle solvent, the SX5000 is the more serious product. For brick walls and chimneys around the home, Masonry Defender is the easier option.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Vs LastiSeal.</strong> LastiSeal takes a different approach: its sealer polymerises inside the pores rather than lining them, and it comes with a 15-year warranty, as our <a href="/lastiseal-reviews/">LastiSeal review</a> explains. That buys a longer warranty in exchange for stricter preparation, since the masonry must be bare and open. Masonry Defender's silane-siloxane products keep the masonry more breathable and are more forgiving of a homeowner's application, but with a 5-year term.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Vs ChimneySaver.</strong> This one is more like a sibling than a rival, because SaverSystems makes both. ChimneySaver is the professional line sold through chimney sweeps, in water-based and solvent-based versions. Retailer listings put the solvent version's warranty at 10 years, double the Masonry Defender chimney product. If a chimney professional is doing the work, ChimneySaver is the natural choice. If you are doing it yourself and buying online, Masonry Defender's Chimney Brick Sealer is the consumer counterpart.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-chimney-sealer/">chimney sealer guide</a> compares the wider field. For pavers, note that the comparison changes, because Masonry Defender's paver product is an acrylic film. Compare it with other wet-look sealers, or look at our <a href="/best-natural-look-paver-sealer/">natural-look paver sealer picks</a> if you want the pavers to look unsealed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Owners Report, and Our Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Masonry Defender does not have a large body of long-term owner reviews. Some listings show only a handful of ratings, too few to draw firm conclusions from. Within that small sample, the comments mostly describe water beading soon after application on the penetrating products, and some mention slight darkening on dense surfaces, which matches the maker's own warning. We found no independent long-term test that names the product, and we have not tested it ourselves.
          </p>

          <p>
            For exterior brick walls and chimneys, the Chimney Brick Sealer or the All Purpose Concrete, Brick & Stone Sealer is a sensible, low-odour choice from a maker with real chimney experience. Apply it at the full coverage rate, wet-on-wet, after the mortar and crown are repaired, and register the warranty. For a concrete driveway or patio, the Concrete Driveway Sealer is a breathable option with a 3-year horizontal warranty, and our <a href="/best-driveway-sealer/">driveway sealer guide</a> sets it against the rest of the concrete category.
          </p>

          <p>
            For interior brick, such as a fireplace surround, the penetrating All Purpose sealer is the wrong product: its data sheet says it is not intended for interior use. The Interior/Exterior Brick Sealer is the one made for that job, but it is an acrylic that adds a satin sheen and darkens the brick, so test it on a hidden spot first. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-seal-fireplace-brick/">sealing fireplace brick</a> covers what to use where heat is involved.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip the line if you want a premium solvent-based concrete treatment, a warranty longer than five years on a wall, or pavers that look untouched. In those cases the Foundation Armor, LastiSeal or a true penetrating paver sealer will suit you better. For everything else, Masonry Defender is a reasonable value choice, provided you read the product name as carefully as the brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by Brand Instead of Product</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Masonry Defender sells both penetrating silane-siloxane repellents and film-forming acrylics. The acrylic brick and paver sealers add sheen and darken the surface. Check the chemistry on the specific product before buying, especially if you want a natural look.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the All Purpose Sealer Indoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The All Purpose Concrete, Brick & Stone data sheet says it is not intended for interior or below-grade use. For a fireplace surround or interior brick wall, the maker's Interior/Exterior Brick Sealer is the product designed for it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Misting Instead of Flooding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The data sheets call for flooding vertical surfaces until the excess runs six to eight inches below the pattern, then a wet-on-wet second coat within minutes. A light mist misses the coverage rate, gives weaker repellency and can void the warranty's minimum-application condition.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging the Result the Same Day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                SaverSystems says repellency develops after 4 to 6 hours of UV and atmospheric moisture and keeps strengthening for several weeks. A splash test on day one can look disappointing. Wait at least a day, and judge the full result after a few weeks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting to Register the Warranty</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The warranty only activates once you register on masonrydefender.com with a copy of your receipt. Paver claims also require the maker's two-step clean-and-seal system. Register on the day you apply, while the receipt is still easy to find.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes Masonry Defender?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                SaverSystems of Richmond, Indiana, which also makes the ChimneySaver line sold through chimney professionals. SaverSystems has supplied the chimney sweep trade since the 1980s, and Masonry Defender is its homeowner brand, sold mainly online. The maker's name and address appear on every Masonry Defender data sheet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Masonry Defender a silane-siloxane sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some of it is. The All Purpose Concrete, Brick & Stone Sealer and the Concrete Driveway Sealer are silane-siloxane, and the Chimney Brick Sealer is a modified siloxane, all penetrating and breathable. The Interior/Exterior Brick Sealer and Patio Paver Sealer are acrylics that form a film, add some sheen and darken the surface.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does Masonry Defender last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The maker warrants a water-repellent effect for five years on vertical surfaces for the All Purpose and Chimney Brick sealers, seven years for the Advanced product, and three years on horizontal surfaces. Actual life depends on exposure and application rate. The warranty provides replacement product only, not labour.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Masonry Defender change the look of brick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The penetrating products are designed not to change colour or appearance on most surfaces, though the maker says dense surfaces may darken slightly and over-application can darken them too. The acrylic Interior/Exterior Brick Sealer and Patio Paver Sealer do darken the surface and add sheen. Always test a hidden area first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much area does a gallon of Masonry Defender cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically 80 to 150 square feet per gallon for the brick and all-purpose products, depending on porosity, and 90 to 150 for the driveway sealer. The paver sealer covers about 75 to 150 square feet on concrete pavers and 150 to 200 on clay pavers. Porous, older brick sits at the low end.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use Masonry Defender on an asphalt driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The data sheets say not to use the products on asphalt-based materials, and to protect asphalt from overspray. Masonry Defender is for concrete, brick and stone. An asphalt driveway needs an asphalt emulsion or similar driveway sealer designed for that material.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Masonry Defender better than Foundation Armor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is different rather than better. Foundation Armor SX5000 is a solvent-based silane-siloxane sold on high active content, with a water-based version available. Masonry Defender's penetrating products are water-based, low-odour and easy to clean up. For heavy-duty concrete, SX5000 is the stronger choice; for brick around the home, Masonry Defender is easier to use.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

